<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Minden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> was fought on 1 August 1759 near Minden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. An Anglo-German army under Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> defeated a French army under the Marquis de Contades, after British and Hanoverian infantry advanced against massed French cavalry and drove it back. The victory saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> from French occupation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-minden.jpg" alt="Battle of Minden" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_The_Battle_of_Minden_in_Westphalia_-_B1995.13.12_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1757 the French invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, won the Battle of Hastenbeck and tried to impose the Convention of Klosterzeven, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> refused to ratify. In 1758 Ferdinand drove the French back across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and beat them at Krefeld, and Britain sent 9,000 troops to reinforce him. In April 1759, however, the duc de Broglie held off his attack at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, and the reinforced French advanced and took the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> on 10 July.</p><p>Contades held a strong position along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>. When Ferdinand split his force to threaten the French supply lines, Contades believed it over-extended and, on the night of 31 July, moved his army onto the plain west of the town. The French had 42,000 infantry, 10,000 cavalry and 162 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> 45,000 men and 170 cannon, among them a British contingent of 10,000 under Lord George Sackville.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Unusually for the time, Contades placed his artillery in the centre, protected only by his cavalry, with his infantry on the flanks. The decisive action came there through a misunderstanding: the British infantry of Friedrich von Spörcken's division, ordered to advance on the beating of drums, took it as an order to advance at once. Under constant artillery fire, the six British regiments, soon supported by two Hanoverian battalions, kept their ranks closed and drove off repeated cavalry charges with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> fire. Contades reportedly said he had seen a single line of infantry break through three lines of cavalry.</p><p>Supported by the British and Hanoverian artillery, the whole Allied line then advanced and sent the French fleeing from the field, with only de Broglie's troops forming a rear guard. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> lost nearly 2,800 men killed and wounded and the French about 7,000. The French retreated south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Sackville was accused of ignoring repeated orders to bring up his cavalry, and a court martial declared him unfit to serve. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the victory was counted part of the Annus Mirabilis of 1759, and George II gave Ferdinand £20,000 and the Order of the Garter. In France Contades was replaced by de Broglie. The successors of the British units, known as the Minden Regiments, mark 1 August as Minden Day by wearing roses, in memory of those the soldiers picked in German gardens on their way to the battle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Minden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Seven Years' War an Anglo-German army under Ferdinand of Brunswick defeated the French nearby at the <b>Battle of Minden</b> on 1 August 1759.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-minden">Principality of Minden</a></li></ul></section>
